{"name":"Feather Plume","hex":"#ffdcb2","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"feather","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A branching, hair-like structure that grows on the bodies of birds, used for flight, swimming, protection and display.","Long hair on the lower legs of a dog or horse, especially a draft horse, notably the Clydesdale breed. Narrowly only the rear hair."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cover or furnish with feathers;  to fletch.","To adorn, as if with feathers; to fringe."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/feather"},{"term":"plume","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A feather of a bird, especially a large or showy one used as a decoration.","A cluster of feathers worn as an ornament, especially on a helmet; a hackle."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To adorn, cover, or furnish with feathers or plumes, or as if with feathers or plumes.","Chiefly of a bird: to arrange and preen the feathers of, specifically in preparation for flight; hence , to prepare for (something)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/plume"}],"entries":[{"title":"Plumage","blocks":[{"text":"Plumage, or feathering, is a layer of feathers that covers a bird and the pattern, colour, and arrangement of those feathers. The pattern and colours of plumage differ between species and subspecies and may vary with sex and age classes. Within a few species, there can be different colour morphs. The placement of feathers on a bird is not haphazard but rather emerges in organised, overlapping rows and groups, and these feather tracts are known by standard names."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Plumage_house_sparrow.jpg/330px-Plumage_house_sparrow.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plumage"},{"title":"Plume (feather)","blocks":[{"text":"A plume is a special type of bird feather, possessed by egrets, ostriches, birds of paradise, quetzals, pheasants, peacocks and quails. They often have a decorative or ornamental purpose, commonly used among marching bands and the military, worn on the hat or helmet of the wearer. Famously by the blues & royals and the Life Guards. When used on military headdresses, the clipped feather plume is referred to as the hackle."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/Cavalier_Garde_R%C3%A9publicaine_trois-quart_dos.jpg/330px-Cavalier_Garde_R%C3%A9publicaine_trois-quart_dos.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plume_(feather)"},{"title":"Feather","blocks":[{"text":"Feathers are epidermal growths that form a distinctive outer covering, or plumage, on both avian (bird) and some non-avian dinosaurs and other archosaurs. They are the most complex integumentary structures found in vertebrates and an example of a complex evolutionary novelty. They are among the characteristics that distinguish the extant birds from other living groups."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/80/Types_de_plumes._-_Larousse_pour_tous%2C_-1907-1910-.jpg/330px-Types_de_plumes._-_Larousse_pour_tous%2C_-1907-1910-.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Feather"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/Mandarinducksum.JPG/500px-Mandarinducksum.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/Mandarinducksum.JPG/500px-Mandarinducksum.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Mandarin duck (male) in eclipse plumage","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMandarinducksum.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/Guiraca_caeruleaAAP086CB.jpg/500px-Guiraca_caeruleaAAP086CB.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/Guiraca_caeruleaAAP086CB.jpg/500px-Guiraca_caeruleaAAP086CB.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The differences in plumage of a blue grosbeak, from top to bottom, between a breeding male (\"alternate\" plumage), a non-breeding male (\"basic\" plumage), a female, and the related indigo bunting.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGuiraca_caeruleaAAP086CB.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/White_Blackbird_DSC01485.jpg/500px-White_Blackbird_DSC01485.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/White_Blackbird_DSC01485.jpg/1280px-White_Blackbird_DSC01485.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/White_Blackbird_DSC01485.jpg/500px-White_Blackbird_DSC01485.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An incomplete leucistic common blackbird.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWhite_Blackbird_DSC01485.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/Albino_crow_and_its_mother.JPG/500px-Albino_crow_and_its_mother.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/Albino_crow_and_its_mother.JPG/1280px-Albino_crow_and_its_mother.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/Albino_crow_and_its_mother.JPG/500px-Albino_crow_and_its_mother.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An albino juvenile house crow in Malacca, Malaysia, next to its normal-coloured parent","link":"h